    "logic": "Glyphs of the title become rigid bodies in a deterministic 2D world and collide by their own outlines, counters and crooks included. Six arrangements share that construction: collapse (the set headline loses letters from the bottom up), pour (the whole title drops into a heap), sign (letters nailed at both upper corners lose nails and swing or fall; the nails stay in the wall), stairs (letters tumble down tipped shelves), garland (letters linked by rings sag between two nails; released letters break the chain) and balance (letters land on a seesaw that tips). Glass letters break where they strike hard enough: Voronoi shards crowd the impact, keep the letter's motion and scatter, and each shard is the real outline clipped to its cells. Solid, outline, cast-shadow and neon looks render the same poses. Empty sockets keep each missing letter's outline, motion exposures repeat a letter along its path, and the chosen moment can catch letters mid-fall.",
    "references": [],
    "basis": "Authored system. Glyph outlines become convex collision pieces; a sequential-impulse solver with friction, restitution and resting-contact stabilisation produces the poses. The simulation runs once when the scene is made and every pose is saved, so renders and vector PDFs replay exactly.",
